# Non-secret App Store Connect settings that don't belong in the Fastfile
# itself. Metadata text and screenshots come from fastlane/metadata and
# fastlane/screenshots by directory convention — nothing to configure for
# those here.

app_identifier("com.chienhunglin.hotkeypad")
default_locale("en-US")

# https://developer.apple.com/app-store/categories/
primary_category("UTILITIES")

# TODO: App Store Connect requires an age rating questionnaire answered
# at least once before a version can be submitted — deliver can't fill
# this in for you the first time. Do it once in the App Store Connect web
# UI (App Information -> Age Rating), it carries forward after that.

# What the reviewer sees on the "App Review Information" screen — spells
# out, in plain terms, why each sensitive permission in Info.plist exists
# (NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription, NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription,
# NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription, NSCameraUsageDescription) so a reviewer
# testing cold doesn't have to guess. Contact fields (first_name,
# last_name, phone_number, email_address) are left unset here rather than
# hard-coded — App Store Connect already carries whatever was last saved
# for the account, and this is personal contact information that
# shouldn't live in a checked-in file anyway.
app_review_information(
  notes: <<~NOTES.strip
    Two Bluetooth permissions and one local-network permission, all for
    the same purpose: finding and talking to this app's own companion
    Mac app, "HotkeyPad Host" (free, not on the App Store — a Stream
    Deck-style desktop app this phone app is the remote control for).

    - Bluetooth (NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription /
      NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription): scans for the Mac's
      HotkeyPad Host advertising a specific GATT service UUID, connects,
      and exchanges button-press messages over it. No pairing/bonding at
      the OS level, and no other Bluetooth accessories are touched.
    - Local Network (NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription): a full alternative
      to Bluetooth on the same WiFi network — the host also advertises
      itself with a small UDP broadcast, and this app listens for it and
      connects over TCP the same way, for the case where Bluetooth is
      unavailable or slower to find it.
    - Camera (NSCameraUsageDescription): only ever used to scan a QR
      code the host app displays on request, as a faster alternative to
      typing its IP address by hand to pair over WiFi. No photo or video
      is captured, stored, or leaves the device.

    There is no account, login, server, or analytics of any kind — the
    phone talks directly, peer-to-peer, to a Mac on the same network.

    To test end-to-end: install the free "HotkeyPad Host" app on a Mac
    on the same network as the test device (or the same WiFi as the
    reviewer's Mac), open it, and this app should find and connect to
    it automatically within a few seconds.
  NOTES
)
